Quick comparison of the two leading open-source relational databases:

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Comparison("PostgreSQL vs MySQL", [pg, mysql])
pg = {name:"PostgreSQL", attributes:["Object-relational (MVCC)","Full ACID","Native JSONB with GIN indexing","Built-in full-text search","Logical + streaming replication","Read replicas; sharding via Citus/pg_shard","PostgreSQL License (permissive)","Best for: complex queries, analytics, strict data integrity"]}
mysql = {name:"MySQL", attributes:["Relational (InnoDB)","Full ACID with InnoDB","JSON type (less rich querying)","FTS in InnoDB (5.7+)","Async replication + Group Replication","Read replicas; sharding app-level","GPL v2 / commercial (Oracle)","Best for: simple CRUD, web apps, read-heavy workloads"]}
:::

**Recommendation:** Choose **PostgreSQL** when you need advanced queries (CTEs, window functions, JSONB), strict integrity, or analytics. Choose **MySQL** for simpler web/CRUD workloads, broad host compatibility, or when your team/ecosystem already standardizes on it. Both are excellent; the gap is smaller than ever.

